Spectral and Source Serif 4 (self-hosted web fonts)
====================================================

Used by the @font-face rules in docs/blog/*.html (the four blog articles
and the blog index) and in docs/de/index.html for --font-display
(Spectral) and --font-body (Source Serif 4). Self-hosted so the pages
carry no external font CDN (Google Fonts or otherwise) — see CLAUDE.md's
font policy.

These two families were fetched once before (commit fc47183) but removed
again by a later, unrelated redesign (commit 241e926) that moved
docs.html and docs/index.html (EN) to Sora + JetBrains Mono and never
touched docs/de/index.html or the older blog scaffold, both of which kept
declaring Spectral/Source Serif 4 by path — docs/de/index.html was never
migrated to the newer design at all (it is also still on version 1.0.0 in
several places; a separate, larger gap than fonts). Every @font-face rule
on both pointed at files that no longer existed for months; the browser
fell back to the declared Georgia fallback, so nothing visibly broke, but
nothing was actually self-hosted either. Refetched here and pinned with
this file so it can't happen again silently; tests/test_v160_design.py's
`test_every_docs_font_face_url_resolves_to_a_real_file` now asserts every
@font-face url() under docs/ resolves to a real file.

Source
------

Both fetched from their official @fontsource npm packages (the same
distribution method the DejaVu font in app/fonts/ documents, and the one
Sora/JetBrains Mono in this same directory already use):

  @fontsource/spectral@5.3.0
    https://registry.npmjs.org/@fontsource/spectral/-/spectral-5.3.0.tgz
    sha512-2TL5WWGOZj4SIsBmPFEzKeA60tXXd1Qp1OqaaVEWkJL8IfSGLk6YmiusmC441iFkdJDIuFelu1r8lhCmQUU3mw==

  @fontsource/source-serif-4@5.3.0
    https://registry.npmjs.org/@fontsource/source-serif-4/-/source-serif-4-5.3.0.tgz
    sha512-yQz8xmIgMzks8zQbpuca3UYtjxK798XCyQAGdDXmzBgvV7sdoK6d0YvE9F9kiYxORCEMRBgGZDZsSYcXj5KvwA==

Both integrity values were checked against the `dist.integrity` field the
npm registry publishes for that exact version (`npm view <pkg>@<version>
dist.integrity`) before any file was copied out of the tarball.

Files taken, unmodified, from each package's files/ directory — only the
weights/styles some page actually declares, nothing extra:

  spectral-latin-400-normal.woff2       blog + de/index.html
  spectral-latin-400-italic.woff2       de/index.html only
  spectral-latin-500-normal.woff2       de/index.html only
  spectral-latin-600-normal.woff2       de/index.html only
  spectral-latin-600-italic.woff2       de/index.html only
  spectral-latin-700-normal.woff2       blog + de/index.html
  spectral-latin-700-italic.woff2       de/index.html only (round 2)
  spectral-latin-ext-400-normal.woff2   blog only
  spectral-latin-ext-600-normal.woff2   blog only (round 2)
  spectral-latin-ext-700-normal.woff2   blog only
  source-serif-4-latin-300-normal.woff2      blog + de/index.html
  source-serif-4-latin-300-italic.woff2      de/index.html only (round 2)
  source-serif-4-latin-400-normal.woff2      blog + de/index.html
  source-serif-4-latin-400-italic.woff2      de/index.html only
  source-serif-4-latin-600-normal.woff2      blog + de/index.html
  source-serif-4-latin-700-normal.woff2      de/index.html only
  source-serif-4-latin-ext-300-normal.woff2  blog only
  source-serif-4-latin-ext-400-normal.woff2  blog only
  source-serif-4-latin-ext-600-normal.woff2  blog only

Round 2 additions: the blog scaffold's own CSS asks for Spectral weight 600
(`.nav-logo`, `.article-body h3`, and blog index's `.footer-logo`), and
docs/de/index.html's CSS asks for Spectral 700 italic
(`.hero-headline .accent-emphasis`, which inherits `.hero-headline`'s
weight 700) and Source Serif 4 300 italic (`.hero-subline em`, which
inherits `.hero-subline`'s weight 300) — none of these were shipped, so the
browser was synthesizing a faux-bold or faux-italic instead of using the
real face. `tests/test_v160_design.py::test_every_docs_page_font_usage_has_a_matching_font_face`
now catches this class of gap (a shipped family missing a specific
weight/style the page's own CSS actually uses), not just "the declared
@font-face url() resolves" (round 1's narrower guard).

Only the blog pages split `latin`/`latin-ext` into separate @font-face
rules with a `unicode-range` each (the exact ranges fontsource publishes
for those subsets) — `latin` alone (U+0000-00FF plus general punctuation)
already covers every character the current German/English blog text uses,
including German umlauts and the „…" quotes, but `latin-ext` (Latin
Extended-A/B, e.g. Polish/Czech/Hungarian diacritics) is shipped and wired
in too, so a future article naming a person or place with those characters
renders in the real typeface instead of silently falling back to Georgia.
docs/de/index.html predates that split; it declares one `latin`-only rule
per weight/style, unchanged here — only the missing files it already
referenced (or, round 2, actually uses) were added, not a redesign of its
CSS.

License
-------

SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1 — full text as shipped by each
package, unmodified: spectral-LICENSE (Spectral, copyright 2017 The
Spectral Project Authors) and source-serif-4-LICENSE (Source Serif 4).
Permissive, attribution-preserving, explicitly allows embedding and
redistribution; compatible with this project's GPLv3.

Sora and JetBrains Mono (docs.html + docs/index.html EN)
==========================================================

Round 1 called `sora-latin-*.woff2`/`JetBrainsMono-*.woff2` "unrelated,
not covered by this fix" — round 2's broader coverage test found the same
class of gap here too: docs/index.html's `.hero-subline`/`.docs-lead`/
`.section-subheading` ask for Sora weight 300 (only 400/500/600/700 were
shipped), and `.t-comment` (inside a code block, so it inherits
`font-family: var(--font-mono)` from its ancestor, not body's Sora) and
docs/index.html's `.footer-logo` ask for JetBrains Mono weight 500 and
weight 400 italic (only 400/700 normal were shipped). Fixed the same way
as Spectral/Source Serif 4:

  @fontsource/sora@5.3.0
    https://registry.npmjs.org/@fontsource/sora/-/sora-5.3.0.tgz
    sha512-SqW3OpKxxfKvxDLPlbdrWf2KooPfbd+W3jaYn483o+Y/JrmU87vjFPNptDqwP428c3vY+gnranLhjcbRaXB0Jw==

  @fontsource/jetbrains-mono@5.3.0
    https://registry.npmjs.org/@fontsource/jetbrains-mono/-/jetbrains-mono-5.3.0.tgz
    sha512-fqDfB5I9f1p1TV486aUgB9t8zP84P0O1FtQR5Ol9vjwPy+S+EIGlVYm1cvj2W5shcZMTg2nZFdVMoH5wFu8a1A==

Verified the *existing* `sora-latin-{400,500,600,700}-normal.woff2` are
byte-identical to this exact package's own files (`cmp`) — same source,
confirmed rather than assumed. New file: `sora-latin-300-normal.woff2`
(docs.html + docs/index.html both use weight 300; no `latin-ext`, matching
the flat single-subset style this family already used here).

**Round 3: `JetBrainsMono-Regular.woff2`/`JetBrainsMono-Bold.woff2`
re-sourced.** Round 2 found they did NOT match `@fontsource/jetbrains-mono`
at all (`cmp` differed at byte 11; they were roughly double the package's
own latin-only 400/700 file sizes) and left them alone as an unverified
prior origin, out of that round's scope. Controller ruling: re-source them
properly rather than carry the gap forward. Re-verified the tarball fresh
(`npm view @fontsource/jetbrains-mono@5.3.0 dist.integrity` against a
freshly downloaded tarball's own sha512 — matches exactly, same value as
below) and replaced both files' bytes with the package's own
`jetbrains-mono-latin-400-normal.woff2`/`jetbrains-mono-latin-700-normal.woff2`
(same target filenames — nothing referencing them needed to change; sizes
dropped from ~39.5 KB to ~20–21 KB each, confirming the previous files
really were a different, larger source). All four `JetBrainsMono-*.woff2`
files in this directory (Regular, Bold, and round 2's Medium/Italic) are
now `cmp`-identical to this one package:

  @fontsource/jetbrains-mono@5.3.0
    https://registry.npmjs.org/@fontsource/jetbrains-mono/-/jetbrains-mono-5.3.0.tgz
    sha512-fqDfB5I9f1p1TV486aUgB9t8zP84P0O1FtQR5Ol9vjwPy+S+EIGlVYm1cvj2W5shcZMTg2nZFdVMoH5wFu8a1A==

  JetBrainsMono-Regular.woff2  (weight 400 normal)
  JetBrainsMono-Bold.woff2     (weight 700 normal)
  JetBrainsMono-Medium.woff2   (weight 500 normal — docs/index.html `.footer-logo`)
  JetBrainsMono-Italic.woff2   (weight 400 italic — docs.html + docs/index.html `.t-comment`)

No `unicode-range` was added: none of these four rules ever declared one
(unlike the blog's Spectral/Source Serif 4 latin/latin-ext split), every
use of this family on every page is plain ASCII (case numbers, PINs,
timestamps, code-comment prose, the "OpenWhistle" wordmark), and adding a
narrow range without a second face to cover what it excludes would only
ever *lose* coverage, never gain any — nothing to "update" that wasn't
already correct.

**These are the same files the running application ships**, not a
separate copy: `app/static/fonts/` does not exist in the repository at
all — the Dockerfile (`COPY --chown=openwhistle:openwhistle
docs/fonts/*.woff2 /app/app/static/fonts/`) copies this exact directory
into the image at build time, and `app/static/css/fonts.css` declares the
identical four `@font-face` rules (400/700 normal only — the app never
uses weight 500 or italic JetBrains Mono) pointing at the same four
filenames. Re-sourcing here fixes both the public docs pages and the
running app in one step; `app/static/css/fonts.css` needed no edit since
the filenames it references didn't change.

License: SIL Open Font License 1.1 for both Sora and JetBrains Mono (same
license family as Spectral/Source Serif 4/DejaVu). Round 2 left both
without a LICENSE file in this directory, flagged as a pre-existing gap;
closed here alongside the JetBrains Mono re-sourcing since the packages
were already being verified anyway: `sora-LICENSE`, `jetbrains-mono-LICENSE`
(both unmodified, full OFL-1.1 text as shipped by their packages).

Sora italic: removed at the source, not shipped as an exception
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Round 2 found three spots on docs/index.html requesting italic Sora
(`.footer-tagline`, `.hero-headline .accent-emphasis`, `.hero-subline
em`) and, since **Sora has no italic member at all** — verified by
extracting the official `@fontsource/sora@5.3.0` package and confirming
its `files/` directory contains zero `*-italic` files, upstream — excused
them in the coverage test rather than leave it permanently red. Round 3's
controller ruling: don't ship (or excuse) a style the family can never
have — remove the request instead. All three rules now use `font-weight`
or an existing `color` token for emphasis in place of `font-style:
italic` (the `.hero-subline em` rule also needed an explicit `font-style:
normal`, since `<em>` is browser-default-italic and simply deleting the
authored italic line would have left the user-agent stylesheet's own
italic in force):

  .footer-tagline              font-style: italic  ->  font-weight: 500 (already shipped)
  .hero-headline .accent-emphasis   font-style: italic  ->  (dropped; var(--accent) color already carries the emphasis)
  .hero-subline em              font-style: italic  ->  font-style: normal; font-weight: 600 (already shipped)

`test_every_docs_page_font_usage_has_a_matching_font_face` no longer
carries any exception list — every (family, weight, style) any docs/ page
uses now has a real, shipped `@font-face`, with nothing excused.
